'''Robert Jacomb-Hood'''{{Efn|His name is sometimes left unhyphenated as '''Robert Jacomb Hood'''.<ref name="charles-driver" /><ref name="journal-society-arts" />}} {{Postnominal|country=GBR|MICE}} (25 January 1822 – 10 May 1900) was a British civil engineer who rose to prominence as the first Resident Engineer of the [[London, Brighton and South Coast Railway]] (LB&SCR), a position he held from the company's amalgamation in 1846 until 1860. During this time, he was responsible for projects including [[London Victoria station]], [[London Bridge station]], and [[Crystal Palace railway station]], as well as a large number of branch lines across the south of England.  

Born to a working-class family in Bedfordshire, Jacomb-Hood's father inherited an estate from his cousin in 1833, drastically improving the financial situation of the family. Jacomb-Hood was removed from [[Christ's Hospital]] in favour of private tuition before attending the [[University of Cambridge]] to study Law. However, he quickly dropped out to become a railway engineer instead, rising through their ranks quickly under the guidance of [[George W. Buck]]. He was selected as the fist Resident Engineer of the LB&SCR in 1846, only five years after he entered the industry. The finances of the company were tumultuous, with some years seeing strong gains and others so poor that Jacomb-Hood narrowly escaped being fired. However, his career personal finances grew steadily and he was elected a Member of the [[Institution of Civil Engineers]] in 1847.  

After transitioning to practising privately in 1860, he began partnering with a variety of engineers and architects including [[Charles Driver]], [[George Parker Bidder (engineer)|George Parker Bidder]], and his cousin Wililam Jacomb. He also expanded his work outside of railway engineering, working on projects such as the [[National Gallery]] and the Portcreek Viaduct. However, the death of his first wife and the end of his joint practice with Jacomb motivated him to enter semi-retirement; as part of this, he switched his focus from designing and constructing projects in the UK to joining companies' [[Board of directors|boards of directors]] and taking up international opportunities. This included significant contributions to the [[The Crystal Palace|Crystal Palace Company]] between 1869 and 1880, as well as the [[Alabama Great Southern Railroad]] which he helped to create and sat as a member of the board between 1877 and 1886.  

In 1883 he returned to the LB&SCR as a member of the board; by this time, he was barely working and instead spending most of his time travelling the world. He slowly left the companies he had been a board member of; his last known attendance at a civil engineering project was in 1894, but he continued assisting the LB&SCR until his death in 1900 aged 78. Over his extensive six-decade career, he saw success not only as the designer and engineer of railway projects, but as the director and important figure of various companies across four continents. He also saw academic success, being awarded two of the Institution of Civil Engineers' accolades in 1850 and 1858 respectfully, the latter of which was for a paper that was considered pioneering in station design and [[railway engineering]].  

== Early life ==

=== Family ===
[[File:St Mary and All Saints Church, Stambridge Road, Great Stambridge (NHLE Code 1168425) (May 2024) (2).jpg|left|thumb|St Mary and All Saints Church in [[Great Stambridge]], where Jacomb-Hood's parents married.|alt=A medieval church surrounded by a cemetery. There are trees in the right foreground and left background, and a blue sky]]
Jacomb-Hood was born on 25 January 1822 at The Lodge Farm in [[Riseley, Bedfordshire]].<ref group="‡">{{Cite web |last=Coates |first=John |date=19 July 1822 |title=Dr Williams' Library Registry, Birth Certificates, 1820–1824 |url=https://www.ancestry.com/imageviewer/collections/2972/images/40612_B0151977-00413 |url-access=subscription |access-date=8 July 2025 |publisher=General Register Office |via=[[Ancestry]] |archive-date=9 August 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250809111254/https://www.ancestry.com/imageviewer/collections/2972/images/40612_B0151977-00413 |url-status=live }}</ref> He was also [[baptised]] in Riseley,<ref group="‡">{{Cite web |title=Robert Jacomb in the England & Wales, Christening Index, 1530-1980 |url=https://www.ancestry.com/search/collections/1351/records/5723667? |url-access=subscription |access-date=8 August 2025 |publisher= |via=[[Ancestry]]}}</ref> with unpublished material from the Christ's Hospital school archives suggesting this was on 21 February 1822.<ref name="christs-hospital-school-london-archives" group="‡">{{Cite book |title=Robert Jacomb-Hood |publisher=[[Christ's Hospital School|Christ's Hospital School London Archives]] |editor-last=Jones |editor-first=Clifford |language=en-GB |access-date=}} Accessed 25 June 2025. </ref> He was the first of his parents' nine children;<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=58}} according to their marriage certificate, they had married on 29 March 1819 at St Mary And All Saints Church in [[Great Stambridge]], Essex.<ref group="‡">{{Cite web |year=1819 |title=Essex, England, Church of England Marriages, 1754–1937 |url=https://www.ancestry.com/search/collections/61700/records/1440217 |url-access=subscription |url-status= |access-date=8 July 2025 |publisher=Essex Record Office |via=[[Ancestry]]}}</ref> 

Jacomb-Hood's mother was Susan Jacomb-Hood ({{Nee|Kemp}}), the daughter of John Kemp of Broomhills in [[Rochford]], Essex.<ref name="walford">{{Cite book |last=Walford |first=Edward |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=n9pv_1xLploC |title=The County Families of the United Kingdom |date=1864 |publisher=Hardwicke |location=London |pages=546 |language=en-GB |via=[[Google Books]] |archive-date=7 June 2025 |access-date=7 June 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250607111638/https://books.google.com/books?id=n9pv_1xLploC |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last=Benton |first=Philip |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=ioYuAAAAMAAJ |title=The History of Rochford Hundred |publisher=A. Harrington |year=1888 |volume=II |location=Rochford |pages=569 |language=en |via=[[Google Books]]}}</ref> His mother's brother—George Tawke Kemp—owned the Beechhill estate, also situated in Rochford, and his son [[George Kemp, 1st Baron Rochdale|George]] was given the title [[Baron Rochdale]] on 14 February 1913.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Burke |first=Bernard |author-link=Bernard Burke |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=InoHD0N_Jp0C |title=A Genealogical and Heraldic History of the Peerage and Baronetage, the Privy Council, Knightage, and Companionage |year=1934 |pages=2020 |language=en-GB |via=[[Google Books]]}}</ref><ref>{{London Gazette
| issue = 28690
| date = 14 February 1913
| page = 1146
}}</ref>

Jacomb-Hood's father was Robert Jacomb, who began his career as a [[Yeoman|yeoman farmer]].<ref name="sellingantiques">{{Cite web |title=Oil Painting Young Lady In Blue Dress Signed George Percy Jacomb-hood |url=https://www.sellingantiques.co.uk/992902/oil-painting-young-lady-in-blue-dress-signed-george-percy-jacombhood |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250607100331/https://www.sellingantiques.co.uk/992902/oil-painting-young-lady-in-blue-dress-signed-george-percy-jacombhood |archive-date=7 June 2025 |access-date=7 June 2025 |website=Selling Antiques}}</ref> As of 1822, he was a [[tenant farmer]] on land belonging to [[John Russell, 6th Duke of Bedford]].<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=51}} On the 1833 death of his cousin, the [[barrister]] William Hood,<ref name=":0">{{Cite book |last=Burke |first=Bernard |author-link=Bernard Burke |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=ZNEKAAAAYAAJ |title=A Genealogical and Heraldic Dictionary of the Landed Gentry of Great Britain and Ireland |date=1875 |publisher=H. Colburn |edition=5th |volume=I |location=London |pages=612 |language=en-GB |archive-date=24 August 2025 |access-date=24 August 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250824080323/https://www.google.co.uk/books/edition/Genealogical_and_Heraldic_Dictionary_of/ZNEKAAAAYAAJ?hl=en&gbpv=0 |url-status=live }}</ref> Jacomb inherited Hood's estate at [[Bardon Hall]] in Bardon, [[Leicestershire]];<ref name=":0" /><ref name="bulletin-of-the-loughborough-and-district-archaeological-society-1981" /> this was because Hood was the last male member of his family. Jacomb had no link to the estate and his interest in its ownership was only because of the financial benefit.<ref name="bulletin-of-the-loughborough-and-district-archaeological-society-1981">{{Cite journal |date=1981 |editor-last=Moffat |editor-first=W.S. |title=Bardon Park History |url=https://www.loughboroughpastandpresent.org/bulletin/bulletin_v2_5.pdf |journal=Bulletin of the Loughborough and District Archaeological Society |volume=2 |issue=8–9 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250729125731/https://www.loughboroughpastandpresent.org/bulletin/bulletin_v2_5.pdf |archive-date=29 July 2025}}</ref> However, this inheritance was on the condition that Jacomb took the surname of Hood, becoming Robert Jacomb-Hood,<ref name=":0" /> because the estate had been in the Hood family since the 1620s.<ref name="sellingantiques" /> Jacomb-Hood modified the estate by building a New Hall in 1835 and demolishing the Old Hall building around 1840,<ref name="bulletin-of-the-loughborough-and-district-archaeological-society-1981" /> which he had described in his memoirs as "too dilapidated for residence, and the situation was low, damp and unhealthy".<ref name="noble-1995">{{cite book |last=Noble |first=Len |title=Bardon Hill: A Source Book, Being a Collection of Papers, Anecdotes and Published Work Concerning the Ancient Enclosure of Bardon Park |date=1 June 1995 |publisher=self-published |isbn=978-0952597803 |location=Ellistown |page=17 |language=en-GB}}</ref> He was accepted to the [[Worshipful Company of Fan Makers]]—one of the 113 [[Livery company|livery companies]] of the [[City of London]]<ref>{{Cite web |date=23 January 2025 |title=Database of Companies and Guilds |url=https://liverycommittee.org/about/livery-companies-and-guilds/livery-companies-database/ |access-date=26 July 2025 |website=Livery Committee |language=en-GB}}</ref>—on 9 January 1832.<ref name="christs-hospital-school-london-archives" group="‡" />
=== Education ===
Jacomb-Hood's first education was by a private tutor in [[Dereham]], Norfolk when he was aged seven.<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=51}} He then entered [[Christ's Hospital]], a [[Public school (United Kingdom)|public school]]{{efn|A public school is a type of fee-charging private school,<ref>{{Cite Collins Dictionary|public school|access-date=29 July 2025}}</ref> but Jacomb-Hood did not pay any of his own tuition due to the nature of Christ's Hospital.<ref name="christs-hospital-school-london-archives" group="‡"/>}} in [[West Sussex]].<ref name="obituary">{{Cite journal |year=1900 |title=Obituary. Robert Jacomb-Hood, 1822–1900 |url=https://www.icevirtuallibrary.com/doi/10.1680/imotp.1900.18799?mobileUi=0 |journal=Minutes of the Proceedings of the Institution of Civil Engineers |volume=142 |issue=1900 |pages=359–361 |doi=10.1680/imotp.1900.18799 |eissn=1753-7843 |via=[[Institution of Civil Engineers|ICE Virtual Library]] |archive-date=7 June 2025 |access-date=6 June 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250607101354/https://www.icevirtuallibrary.com/doi/10.1680/imotp.1900.18799?mobileUi=0 |url-status=live }}</ref> <ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=51}} Unpublished archive material stored by the school suggests that Jacomb-Hood's father applied for his place in December 1831, by which time he had three siblings. His father's income at the time of the application was reported to "not exceed £100 per annum",<ref name="christs-hospital-school-london-archives" group="‡" /> which equates to a salary of below £{{Inflation|index=UK|value=100|start_year=1831|r=0|fmt=c}} in {{Inflation/year|index=UK}}.{{Inflation/fn|UK}} Jacomb-Hood was admitted to the school on 13 January 1832, aged nine. His family did not pay for his education or any associated fees; instead, he was sponsored by George Green, the governor for donations at the school. Starting at the preparatory school in [[Hertford]], Hertfordshire,<ref name="christs-hospital-school-london-archives" group="‡" /> Jacomb-Hood then moved to the main school in [[Newgate Street, London]] in 1834.<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=51}}<ref name="christs-hospital-school-london-archives" group="‡" /> 

According to the school archives, of the three departments at the school, Jacomb-Hood belonged to the Writing School, which was for children wanting to pursue financial or commercial work. There, he was considered best out of 258 students for [[penmanship]] on 25 November 1834,<ref name="christs-hospital-school-london-archives" group="‡" /> for which he was awarded a golden pen, the school's equivalent of first prize.<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=51}} As a result of his father's inheritance of Bardon Hall, he was removed from the school on 6 November 1835;<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=51}}<ref name="christs-hospital-school-london-archives" group="‡" /> he was signed out by a 'J A Kemp', who would have been on his mother's side of the family. None of his siblings attended the school.<ref name="christs-hospital-school-london-archives" group="‡" />

After leaving Christ's Hospital, Jacomb-Hood was educated by [[The Reverend|Reverend]] H. Richardson, who was the [[Rector (ecclesiastical)|rector]] of [[Leire]], Leicestershire. However, Jacomb-Hood's father was not happy with the quality of his tuition and moved him to Wood & Throwgood School in [[Totteridge]], Hertfordshire in 1836. By this time, the family were living in Bardon Park and his father was undertaking renovation works. Between 1839 and 1840, Jacomb-Hood was educated in [[Hathern]], Leicestershire, as a precursor to him studying at the [[University of Cambridge]]. At the same time, the family moved to [[Cheltenham]], Gloucestershire; in both locations, Jacomb-Hood witnessed railway construction; those were the [[Birmingham and Gloucester Railway]] in Cheltenham and the [[Midland Counties Railway]] in Hathern. In his diaries, he recalls visiting the sites with his friends, and that they served as an inspiration for his choice of career.<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=51–2}}

Jacomb-Hood's father wanted him to pursue a career as a [[barrister]], and had discouraged his son's interest in engineering; at first, Jacomb-Hood respected his father's wishes, choosing to study [[Law degree|law]].<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=51–2}} He was admitted to [[Trinity College, Cambridge]] on 25 May 1840 and he began his degree the same year.<ref name="venn-1947" /> He is listed in ''[[Alumni Cantabrigienses]]'' as a pensioner (full fee-paying student) and described to be of "of Bardon Park, Leics".<ref name="venn-1947">{{Cite book |last1=Venn |first1=John |author-link1=John Venn |url=https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/alumni-cantabrigienses/1E9370B7DF4BD0905030139A881D9A27 |title=Alumni Cantabrigienses |last2=Venn |first2=John Archibald |editor-first1=John |editor-last1=Venn |author-link2=John Archibald Venn |publisher=[[Cambridge University Press]] |year=1947 |isbn= |editor-first= |series= |volume=2 |location= |pages=543 |doi=10.1017/CBO9781139093958 |language=en-GB |access-date=7 June 2025 |editor-last2=Venn |editor-first2=John Archibald |url-access=subscription |archive-date=7 June 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250607111638/https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/alumni-cantabrigienses/1E9370B7DF4BD0905030139A881D9A27 |url-status=live }}</ref> However, after what Alan A. Jackson describes as a "couple of unsuccessful terms", Jacomb-Hood dropped out of the University in April 1841,<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=52}} instead choosing to become a railway engineer.<ref name="obituary" /><ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=52}}

== Career ==

=== 1841–1846: Early career ===
After dropping out of University, Jacomb-Hood scheduled an interview with civil engineer [[Charles Fox (engineer, born 1810)|Charles Fox]] to join his company Fox & Henderson. However, his former classmate from his education in Hathern, Albert Pell, had been employed at the company; Pell warned Jacomb-Hood to avoid working as an engineer for Fox. He then tried to get a job from [[John Birkinshaw]], who worked under [[Robert Stephenson]] on the [[Birmingham and Derby Junction Railway]]; however, Birkinshaw was ill and thus unable to meet Jacomb-Hood, something which the latter described in his diaries as "fortunate".<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=52}} Finally, he managed successfully to become the student of the railway engineer [[George W. Buck]], who at the time was the Chief Engineer of the [[Manchester and Birmingham Railway]] (M&BR) alongside Robert Stephenson.<ref name="obituary" /><ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=52}} 

Upon becoming a student of Buck, he was tasked to work under [[draftsman]] Charles Heard Wild to make the technical drawings for the railway for a period of nine months. In his diaries, Jacomb-Hood speaks of both Wild and Buck very highly, describing them as "'one of the cleverest draftsmen" and "a most excellent master" respectively.<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=52}} He was then sent by Buck to [[Sandbach]] to work as the assistant to William Baker,<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=52}} who was responsible for a stretch of what would become the [[Crewe–Manchester line]] near [[Holmes Chapel railway station|Holmes Chapel]].<ref name="obituary" /> By the time the line reached [[Crewe railway station|Crewe]] in 1843, Buck had stopped working for the M&BR and was instead practicing privately in Manchester; he then employed Jacomb-Hood as his Chief Assistant.<ref name="obituary" /><ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=52}}[[File:Salford Central Railway Bridge - geograph.org.uk - 1414987.jpg|thumb|The [[Salford Central railway station|Salford Central]] railway bridges, which Jacomb-Hood engineered.|alt=A photograph of a bridge over a road with cars and people on the road. The bridge is built with repeating patterns of red, black, and white.]]
During his time as Chief Assistant to Buck, Jacomb-Hood was tasked with projects including the design and construction of the [[Salford Central railway station|Salford Central]] bridges over New Bailey Road, replacing an older version.<ref name="obituary" /><ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=52}} In late 1843, Jacomb-Hood was employed by the M&BR again as an assistant to Baker, to work on the section of the railway between [[Macclesfield railway station|Macclesfield]] and [[Cheadle Hulme railway station|Cheadle Hulme]] (now part of the [[Stafford–Manchester line]]).<ref name="obituary" /><ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=52}} He began working on the line on 25 December 1843, and carried on through the first few months of 1844.<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=52}} 

After he finished his work for the M&BR, he was then tasked with managing the parliamentary work of the [[Manchester, South Junction and Altrincham Railway]],<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=52}} before being promoted to Resident Engineer of the railway from 1 September 1844.<ref name="obituary" /><ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=|pages=52–53}} It is now known as the [[Altrincham Line]], which forms part of [[Manchester Metrolink]].<ref>{{cite web |title=An Introduction to Metrolink |url=http://lrta.org/Manchester/index.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161028232331/http://lrta.org/Manchester/index.html |archive-date=28 October 2016 |access-date=26 July 2025 |publisher=Light Rail Transit Association}}</ref> He kept this role for two years until 1846.<ref name="obituary" /> During his time as Resident Engineer he also worked with Baker on the [[Shropshire Union Canal]] and the Wilmslow & Knutsford Railway. However, he then turned down offers to work on the [[Leeds Northern Railway]] and to start a partnership with Baker, instead deciding to apply for the new role as Resident Engineer on the [[London, Brighton and South Coast Railway]].<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=|pages=53}}

=== 1846–1850: Early LB&SCR work ===
On 27 July 1846, the London, Brighton and South Coast Railway formed as an amalgamation of companies including the [[London and Brighton Railway]] and the [[London and Croydon Railway]].<ref name="science-museum-group" /> Earlier that year on 6 May, Jacomb-Hood had attended a meeting of the provisional board as a candidate for the company's Resident Engineer. The sixty-two applicants were reduced to a shortlist of three,<ref name="jackson-1996">{{Cite journal |last=Jackson |first=Alan A |date=March 1996 |title=The 'Diaries' of Robert Jacomb Hood, Railway Engineer |url=https://rchs.org.uk/wp-content/uploads/2020/12/Journal-163-Mar-1996.pdf#page=51 |journal=[[Journal of the Railway and Canal Historical Society]] |volume=32 |issue=163 |via=[[Railway and Canal Historical Society]]}}</ref>{{Reference page|page=53}} from which Jacomb-Hood was chosen and appointed from the company's creation.<ref name="science-museum-group">{{Cite web |title=London, Brighton & South Coast Railway Co |url=https://collection.sciencemuseumgroup.org.uk/people/ap253/london-brighton-south-coast-railway-co |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210728081422/https://collection.sciencemuseumgroup.org.uk/people/ap253/london-brighton-south-coast-railway-co |archive-date=28 July 2021 |access-date=7 June 2025 |website=[[Science Museum Group]] |language=en-GB}}</ref><ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=53}} He had a starting salary of £500,<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=53}} equivalent to £{{Inflation|index=UK|value=500|start_year=1846|r=-3|fmt=c}} in {{Inflation/year|index=UK}},{{Inflation/fn|UK}} for which he was responsible for the construction, maintenance, and planning of the lines, stations, and goods depots.<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=53}}
[[File:Steyning railway station 1861.jpg|alt=A black and white photo of a medium-sized brick building as taken from across the street. A canopy extends from the first floor with a white sign reading "STEYNING STATION"|thumb|A photograph of [[Steyning railway station]] on the [[Steyning Line]], taken on opening day in 1861.]]
In his first years at the company, his work mainly focussed on the completion of stations, managing contracts,<ref name="obituary" /> and the railway's new stations at [[London Bridge station|London Bridge]] and [[Bricklayers Arms railway station|Bricklayers Arms]].<ref name="obituary" /><ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=53}} He also designed and oversaw the construction of many branch lines, such as the [[Cuckoo Line]], the [[Seaford branch line|Seaford branch]], and the [[Steyning Line]].<ref name="obituary" /><ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=53}} This was combined with the slow expansion of the [[Brighton Main Line]] down to—and then along—the [[South Coast of England|South Coast]].<ref name="obituary" /><ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=53}} Jacomb-Hood was tasked with further expansion works during 1847, including the completion of the [[Epsom Downs Branch]] in order to serve the upcoming [[Epsom Derby]],<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=53}} which took place that year on 19 May.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Epsom Derby Winners and What day was the Derby run each year? |url=https://eehe.org.uk/24073/epsomderbywinners/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250321031927/https://eehe.org.uk/24073/epsomderbywinners/ |archive-date=21 March 2025 |access-date=2025-08-12 |website=Epsom & Ewell History Explorer |language=en-GB}}</ref> 

On 2 March 1847, Jacob-Hood was elected a Member of the [[Institution of Civil Engineers]] (MICE).<ref name="obituary" /> From May of the same year, Jacomb-Hood took in his brother George Jacomb-Hood as a pupil; the pair lived together in rented accommodation in [[Brighton]].<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=53}} His work, however, did not always involve construction; for example, he was also tasked with converting the [[Atmospheric railway#London and Croydon Railway|Atmospheric Railway]]—which had originally been built by the [[London and Croydon Railway]]<ref name="brennan-2020">{{Cite web |last=Brennan |first=Joseph |date=2020 |title=The Atmospheric Road: Croydon |url=https://www.columbia.edu/~brennan/atmo/06_CROYDON.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250515025409/https://www.columbia.edu/~brennan/atmo/06_CROYDON.html |archive-date=15 May 2025 |access-date=12 August 2025 |website=[[Columbia University]]}}</ref>—to steam running rather than by the use of pressure.<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=53}} The conversion finished on 4 May.<ref name="brennan-2020" /> The rate of construction led to his salary (excluding  bonuses) being increased to £750—equivalent to £{{Inflation|index=UK|value=750|start_year=1846|r=-3|fmt=c}} in {{Inflation/year|index=UK}}{{Inflation/fn|UK}}—from 1 July 1847.<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=53}}

The economic success of Jacomb-Hood and the LB&SCR did not last long; by October 1847, Jacomb-Hood was set to be fired—along with the entire of his staff—due to the railway company being unable to find the money to complete more construction work. He gave his [[resignation]] letter to the company on 3 January 1848, but it was overturned by a meeting of the board on 14 February as part of a wider investigation into the conduct and finances of the company. On 18 March, [[Leo Schuster]]—a board member—confirmed to Jacomb-Hood that he would be reappointed at the same salary, and his work was to be gradually resumed.<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=53}} 

On 10 January 1849, Jacomb-Hood was appointed as the engineer to the [[Portsmouth line]], which was a joint project between the LB&SCR and the [[London and South Western Railway]] (LSWR). The same year he also began giving out the contracts for London Bridge station, which first opened on 2 August 1850. Earlier that year, on 2 April, Jacomb-Hood had presented his paper titled ''Description of a Vertical Lift Bridge erected over the Grand Surrey Canal{{Efn|The full title was ''Description of a Vertical Lift Bridge, erected over the Grand Surrey Canal, on the line of the Thames Junction Branch of the London, Brighton, and South Coast Railway.''<ref name="ice-bridge" group="‡"/>}}'' to the Institution of Civil Engineers; the paper details how he engineered the LB&SCR's [[swing bridge]] over the [[Grand Surrey Canal]] in London. The project, which ran between October 1848 and June 1849, had a total cost of £1300, which is equivalent to £{{Inflation|index=GBP|value=1300|start_year=1848|r=-3|fmt=c}} in {{Inflation year|index=GBP}}.{{Inflation/fn|UK}}<ref name="ice-bridge" group="‡">{{Cite journal |last=Jacomb-Hood |first=Robert |year=1850 |title=Description of a Vertical Lift Bridge, Erected Over the Grand Surrey Canal, on the Line of the Thames Junction Branch of the London, Brighton and South Coast Railway. |url=https://www.icevirtuallibrary.com/doi/10.1680/imotp.1850.24166 |journal=Minutes of the Proceedings of the Institution of Civil Engineers |volume=9 |issue=1850 |pages=303–309 |doi=10.1680/imotp.1850.24166 |eissn=1753-7843 |url-access=subscription |via=[[Institution of Civil Engineers|ICE Virtual Library]] |archive-date=7 June 2025 |access-date=6 June 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250607111638/https://www.icevirtuallibrary.com/doi/10.1680/imotp.1850.24166 |url-status=live }}</ref> The paper later won him a Council Premium of Books,<ref name="obituary" /> which he was awarded by the Institution of Civil Engineers on 7 November 1850.<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=54}}

=== 1850–1860: Expansion of the LB&SCR ===
In the 1850s, the LB&SCR saw significant expansion and financial success.<ref name="obituary" />  Due to the financial success of the railway, Jacomb-Hood's pay continued to increase and from 1852 he received an annual bonus proportionate to the [[Dividend|dividends]] of the company, and began investing. On 24 May 1852, Jacomb-Hood attended the first meeting of the Crystal Palace Board, the company responsible for the site in [[Sydenham, London|Sydenham]], London onto which the [[The Crystal Palace]] would be relocated. The board decided that a railway should be incorporated into the site;<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=54}} Jacomb-Hood was made responsible for the construction of this branch by the LB&SCR.<ref name="obituary" /><ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=54}} The same year, he also began the enlargement of [[London Bridge station]] and the construction of [[Lewes railway station]], and the company moved into the new offices at the expanded London Bridge on 27 March 1854.<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|pages=54–55}} He was also tasked by the LB&SCR with the construction of branch lines such as the [[Arun Valley line]] and the [[Oxted line]].<ref name="obituary" /> 

In February 1854, Jacomb-Hood hired [[Guilford Lindsey Molesworth]] as his assistant;<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=55}} Molesworth would later serve as the 41st President of the Institution of Civil Engineers from November 1904 to November 1905.<ref name="Civ2">{{cite book |last=Watson |first=Garth |url=https://archive.org/details/civilsstoryofins0000wats |title=The Civils |date=1988 |publisher=[[Thomas Telford]] |isbn=0-7277-0392-7 |page=252 |url-access=registration}}</ref> On 10 June 1850, Jacomb-Hood attended the opening of The Crystal Palace on its new site with his wife, having been responsible for the construction of the railway line serving it and later becoming a director on the board.<ref name="obituary" /><ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=54}} By 1855, Jacomb-Hood's salary had reached £1059,<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=55}} equivalent to £{{Inflation|index=UK|value=1059|start_year=1855|r=-3|fmt=c}} in {{Inflation/year|index=UK}}.{{Inflation/fn|UK}} In July of the same year, he also negotiated working privately on a new railway between Lewes and Uckfield (now part of the [[Wealden Line]]); he would start this work on 7 August 1858 and would open on 11 October of the same year.<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=56}} The financial success of the LB&SCR—and that of Jacomb-Hood—continued through to the end of the 1850s; he was promised a salary of £1,200 from 1860, equivalent to £{{Inflation|index=UK|value=1200|start_year=1855|r=-3|fmt=c}} in {{Inflation/year|index=UK}}.{{Inflation/fn|UK}} He also hired his younger brother John to work alongside him, tasking him with work in [[Portsmouth]] and [[Newcastle upon Tyne|Newcastle]] before finding him a job in [[Mumbai|Bombay]], India (now Mumbai) in October 1858.<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=56}}
{{Quote box
| quote = Many difficulties arise from the practice of employing architects to design and execute the station works. If, therefore, the Engineer desires to do full credit to his employers, and to himself, he should in all cases design the station works, either engaging assistants, or working conjointly with an architect, to furnish the necessary amount of architectural decoration, a talent for which is not always combined with constructive ability, or the faculty of judicious arrangement.<ref name="collaborators" /><ref name="paper" group="‡" />
| author = Robert Jacomb-Hood
| source = ''The Arrangement and Construction of Railway Stations'' (1858)
| align = left
| width = 425px
| salign = 
}}
On 27 April 1858, Jacomb-Hood submitted a paper to the Institution of Civil Engineers titled ''The Arrangement and Construction of Railway Stations''.<ref name="collaborators">{{Cite book |last1=Herbert |first1=Gilbert |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=XczOCwAAQBAJ |title=The Collaborators: Interactions in the Architectural Design Process |last2=Donchin |first2=Mark |date=23 March 2016 |publisher=[[Taylor & Francis]] |isbn=978-1-317-03790-3 |pages=29 |language=en |url-access=limited |via=[[Google Books]]}}</ref><ref name="paper" group="‡">{{Cite journal |last=Jacomb-Hood |first=Robert |date=27 April 1858 |title=On the Arrangement and Construction of Railway Stations |url=https://www.emerald.com/jmipi/article-abstract/17/1858/449/414849/ON-THE-ARRANGEMENT-AND-CONSTRUCTION-OF-RAILWAY?redirectedFrom=PDF |journal=Minutes of the Proceedings of the Institution of Civil Engineers |volume=17 |issue=1858 |pages=449–481 |doi=10.1680/imotp.1858.23737 |issn=1753-7843 |via=[[Institution of Civil Engineers|ICE Virtual Library]]}}</ref> In this paper, Jacomb-Hood argued that despite the average British railway engineer having little architectural training, the design of stations should nonetheless be their professional responsibility. He proposed the concept of 'judicious arrangement'; that is, that the functionality and logistics of a station was more important to its design than architectural grandeur. His assertion—based on this idea—that railway engineers should design their own stations, was highly innovative and unusual for the time.<ref name="collaborators" /> On 14 December 1858, he was awarded a [[Telford Medal]] by the Institution of Civil Engineering for his work on the paper.<ref name="journal-society-arts">{{Cite journal |last1=Berington |last2=Chadwick |first2=E. |last3=Cole |first3=H. |last4=Graham |first4=Peter |last5=Mechi |first5=J. J. |last6=Morley |first6=Samuel |last7=Nicholay |first7=J. A. |last8=Travers |first8=J. Ingram |last9=Le Neve Foster |first9=P. |date=9 July 1858 |title=Journal of the Society of Arts |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/41323670 |journal=The Journal of the Society of Arts |volume=6 |issue=294 |pages=537 |issn=2049-7865 |jstor=41323670 |via=[[JSTOR]]}}</ref><ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=56}}[[File:London Victoria station -14Oct2008.jpg|thumb|The façade of the LB&SCR half of [[London Victoria station|London Victoria railway station]]; the building was designed by Jacomb-Hood.|alt=A large and tall Victorian building with a canopy extending from the first floor which reads "London Victoria Station" on its left hand side. A road curves around to serve the front of the building and there is a bus on the road and people on the pavement.]]One of Jacomb-Hood's most prominent projects was the construction of [[London Victoria station|London Victoria railway station]], which was to be the shared London terminus of the LB&SCR and the [[London, Chatham and Dover Railway]] (LC&DR). The station was built from two seemingly-unconnected buildings and financed equally by both railway companies.<ref>{{cite book |last=Jackson |first=Alan |title=London's Termini |publisher=[[David & Charles]] |year=1984 |isbn=0-330-02747-6 |edition=New Revised |location=London |pages=267–268 |orig-year=1969}}</ref> Jacomb-Hood was responsible for the design of the LB&SCR's half of the station; by applying his principle of judicious arrangement to the roof—built with a single truss design—the cost of the roof was reduced to £17 per {{Convert|100|sqft|m2}}—£{{Inflation|index=UK|value=17|start_year=1860|r=-2|fmt=c}} in {{Inflation/year|index=UK}}{{Inflation/fn|UK}}—at a cost only two-thirds as expensive as the LC&DR's roof on the other side.<ref name="swailes-2005">{{Cite journal |last1=Swailes |first1=T. |last2=Marsh |first2=J. |date=2005 |title=Development of Long-Span Iron Roof Structures in Britain |url=http://www.emerald.com/jstbu/article/158/5/321-339/441673 |journal=Proceedings of the Institution of Civil Engineers - Structures and Buildings |language=en-GB |volume=158 |issue=5 |pages=335 |doi=10.1680/stbu.2005.158.5.321 |issn=0965-0911 |url-access=subscription}}</ref> The station opened to passengers on 1 October 1860;<ref>{{Cite web |title=The History of London Victoria Station |url=https://www.networkrail.co.uk/who-we-are/our-history/iconic-infrastructure/the-history-of-london-victoria-station/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250405002910/https://www.networkrail.co.uk/who-we-are/our-history/iconic-infrastructure/the-history-of-london-victoria-station/ |archive-date=5 April 2025 |access-date=6 June 2025 |website= |publisher=[[Network Rail]] |language=en-GB}}</ref> the roof does not survive today.<ref name="swailes-2005" />

=== 1860–1869: Full-time private practice ===
Alan A Jackson describes 1860 as a "milestone year" for Jacomb-Hood;<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=57}} on 29 September, Jacomb-Hood resigned as Resident Engineer due to the pressure of managing the parliamentary side of railway engineering.<ref name="obituary" /><ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=57}}{{Efn|In order to purchase the land and raise the capital for a railway project, an act known as a private act had to be passed through the [[Parliament of the United Kingdom]].<ref name="private-acts">{{Cite web|title=Private Acts|publisher=[[UK Parliament]] |url=https://www.parliament.uk/about/living-heritage/transformingsociety/transportcomms/roadsrail/overview/privateacts/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250614193711/https://www.parliament.uk/about/living-heritage/transformingsociety/transportcomms/roadsrail/overview/privateacts/ |archive-date=14 June 2025 |access-date=31 July 2025}}</ref> These were incredibly time consuming and expensive;<ref name="private-acts" /><ref name="full-steam-ahead">{{Cite web|title=Full Steam Ahead |publisher=[[UK Parliament]] |url=https://www.parliament.uk/about/living-heritage/transformingsociety/transportcomms/roadsrail/overview/fullsteamahead/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250326034632/https://www.parliament.uk/about/living-heritage/transformingsociety/transportcomms/roadsrail/overview/fullsteamahead/ |archive-date=26 March 2025 |access-date=31 July 2025}}</ref> for example, [[Isambard Kingdom Brunel]] appeared before parliamentary committees 233 times in his career.<ref name="full-steam-ahead" />}} Instead, he chose to start practising privately as a civil engineer in [[Westminster|Westminster, London]], but agreed to work on his ongoing projects until 31 December 1860 in return for a fee of £3,500<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=57}} (equivalent to £{{Inflation|index=UK|value=3500|start_year=1860|r=-3|fmt=c}} in {{Inflation/year|index=UK}}).{{Inflation/fn|UK}} During his time in private practice, Jacomb-Hood worked on The [[Arun Valley line]], [[South London line]], and [[Cranleigh line]] among others.<ref name="obituary" /> From 1860 to 1863, he worked with the British architect [[Charles Driver]] on projects including the [[Sutton and Mole Valley lines|Dorking to Leatherhead line]], and [[Portsmouth & Southsea railway station|Portsmouth & Southsea]] and [[Tunbridge Wells railway station|Tunbridge Wells]] railway stations.<ref name="charles-driver">{{Cite journal |last=Dobraszczyk |first=Paul |author-link=Paul Dobraszczyk |year=2006 |title=Historicizing Iron: Charles Driver and the Abbey Mills Pumping Station (1865–68) |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/40033824 |journal=[[Architectural History (journal)|Architectural History]] |volume=49 |pages=224 |doi=10.1017/S0066622X0000277X |jstor=40033824 |issn=0066-622X |url-access=subscription |via=[[JSTOR]]}}</ref> Having been employed by his brother in various ways (likely as a result of [[nepotism]]<!-- This is not OR or speculation; it is mentioned in the source -->), George Jacomb-Hood hosted a farewell party on 24 November 1863 before leaving the country to engineer the [[Scinde Railway]] in India, ending his pupilage.<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=58}} 

In his early years of practising privately, Jacomb-Hood also collaborated with the engineer [[George Parker Bidder (engineer)|George Parker Bidder]] on the construction of various projects.<ref name="obituary" /><ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=57}} Despite the fact that Jacomb-Hood had left direct employment of the LB&SCR only a few years prior, the pair assumed all of the company's engineering works contractually from November 1863, splitting their profits evenly.<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=58}} On 29 July 1864, the [[Horsham, Dorking and Leatherhead Railway#Horsham, Leatherhead and Dorking Railway|Horsham, Leatherhead and Dorking Railway]] was absorbed by the LB&SCR; the pair then finished the construction of the line together before it opened on 1 May 1867.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Grant |first=Donald |author-link= |title=Directory of the Railway Companies of Great Britain |publisher=Matador |year=2017 |isbn=978-1785893537 |pages=268}}</ref> The pair also worked on the [[Portsmouth line]] between [[Peckham Rye railway station|Peckham Rye]] and [[Sutton railway station (London)|Sutton]] railway stations;<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=57}} the works on the section had finished by June 1867.<ref>{{Cite journal |date=15 June 1867 |title=Railway Times & Joint-Stock Chronicle |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=VwpCAQAAIAAJ |journal=The Railway Times |language=en-GB |location=London |publisher=Railway Times and Joint-Stock Chronicle Office |volume=30 |issue=1536 |pages=608 |via=[[Google Books]]}}</ref> They also worked on plans for the East London Railway (now partially the [[East London line]]) and the [[Axminster and Lyme Regis Railway]], among many others.<ref name="obituary" />
[[File:Robert Jacomb-Hood.webp|left|thumb|1865 [[lithograph]] of Jacomb-Hood|alt=A lithograph with only grey and cream colours. It features a man wearing a three-piece suit and bow tie leaning to his left; his left hand is on a chair and his right tucked into his blazer.]]
In 1865, a portrait of Jacomb-Hood was made by the [[Lithography|lithographer]] George B. Black; it is part of the [[National Portrait Gallery, London|National Portrait Gallery]]'s collection.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Robert Jacomb-Hood |url=https://www.npg.org.uk/collections/search/portrait/mw193953/Robert-Jacomb-Hood |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250627161346/https://www.npg.org.uk/collections/search/portrait/mw193953/Robert-Jacomb-Hood |archive-date=27 June 2025 |access-date=6 June 2025 |website= |publisher=[[National Portrait Gallery (London)|National Portrait Gallery]] |language=en |publication-place=}}</ref> On 3 July of the same year, Jacomb-Hood entered into a partnership with his cousin and former student William Jacomb;<ref name="obituary" /><ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=58}} the pair practised in [[Little George Street]] in the [[City of Westminster]], and branded their partnership as 'Jacomb and Hood'.<ref name="london-gazette-1869">{{Cite journal |date=1 October 1869 |title=The London Gazette |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=PCZKAQAAMAAJ |journal=[[The London Gazette]] |language=en-GB |publisher=T. Neuman |issue=23542 |pages=7483 |via=[[Google Books]] |archive-date=7 June 2025 |access-date=7 June 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250607111638/https://books.google.com/books?id=PCZKAQAAMAAJ |url-status=live }}</ref> The agreement between them was that Jacomb would provide one third of the necessary capital in return for one third of the profits.<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=58}} Together, they worked on projects across the United Kingdom, for example the Chichester and Midhurst Railway, which formed part of the [[Midhurst Railways]], and the [[Bexleyheath line]].<ref name="obituary" />

In December 1865, Jacomb-Hood travelled to Edinburgh with the intention of entering the competition to design [[Edinburgh Waverley railway station]].<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=58}} However, the station redesign was instead carried out by James Bell, the Chief Engineer of the [[North British Railway]], based on a design for an extension he submitted in 1869. Due to the station remaining open during construction, these changes were not fully implemented until the 1870s.<ref>{{Cite web |title=The history of Edinburgh Waverley station |url=https://www.networkrail.co.uk/who-we-are/our-history/iconic-infrastructure/the-history-of-edinburgh-waverly-station/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250703190830/https://www.networkrail.co.uk/who-we-are/our-history/iconic-infrastructure/the-history-of-edinburgh-waverly-station/ |archive-date=3 July 2025 |access-date=13 August 2025 |website=[[Network Rail]] |language=en-GB}}</ref>

On 28 April 1866, a bridge being built in [[Sutton, London]] as part of Jacomb-Hood's work collapsed, killing six men. On 29 May of the same year, the LB&SCR stopped all its work due to the railway company being in financial difficulties, and Jacomb-Hood was instructed to withdraw or suspend all of the company's parliamentary proposals. However, he was still able to continue his work with the [[South Eastern Railway (England)|South Eastern Railway]] and the [[London, Chatham and Dover Railway]], as well as assisting negotiations between the [[Great Northern Railway (Great Britain)|Great Northern Railway]] and the [[Edgware, Highgate and London Railway]].<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=58}} The financial situation of the LB&SCR had not improved by 1867, and the company descended into chaos with the appointment of an entirely new board of directors; this significantly jeopardised the company's abilities to pass legislation and continue constructing lines.<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=59}}

In the late 1860s Jacomb-Hood also engaged in civil engineering works not directly related to the railways;<ref name="obituary" /> in 1867, he helped to complete the [[ironwork]] on the roof of the [[National Gallery]] building in London alongside his partner William Jacomb.<ref name="obituary" /><ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=59}} The pair were then tasked by the [[War Office]] (now the [[Ministry of Defence (United Kingdom)|Ministry of Defence]]<ref name="NationalArchives-C259">{{Cite web |date=1568–2007 |title=Records created or inherited by the War Office, Armed Forces, Judge Advocate General, and related bodies |url=https://Discovery.NationalArchives.gov.uk/details/r/C259 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250708073249/https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/r/C259 |archive-date=8 July 2025 |access-date=15 August 2025 |publisher=[[The National Archives (United Kingdom)|The National Archives]]}} </ref>) in 1868 to plan repairs to the Portcreek Viaduct near [[Portsmouth]], Hampshire; the works were then carried out during the following year.<ref name="obituary" /><ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=|pages=59–60}} In December 1868, Jacomb-Hood also helped with the inspection of the harbour at [[St Just in Penwith]], Cornwall.<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=|pages=59}} 

In May 1869, Jacomb-Hood was given the title of Consulting Engineer at the LB&SCR in order for him to assist his successor, [[Frederick Banister]], in his work; Jacomb-Hood then worked on once again reorganising the engineering department at the railway. In December 1869, William Jacomb was hired as the Resident Engineer of the [[London and South Western Railway]], because of which their partnership was dissolved on 31 December 1869 by mutual consent;<ref name="london-gazette-1869" /><ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=60}} he would hold this position from the start of 1870 until 1887.<ref name="obituary" /><ref>{{cite book |last=Williams |first=R. A. |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=NVqYzwEACAAJ |title=The London & South Western Railway |publisher=[[David & Charles]] |year=1973 |isbn=978-0715359402 |edition=1st |volume=II: Growth and Consolidation |location=Newton Abbot |via=[[Google Books]] |archive-date=7 June 2025 |access-date=7 June 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250607111638/https://books.google.com/books?id=NVqYzwEACAAJ |url-status=live }}</ref> Combined with the death of his wife the same year (see [[#Personal life|§ Personal life]]), this motivated Jacomb-Hood to end full time practice and enter semi-retirement, moving to a smaller office in Westminster.<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=60}}

=== 1869–1883: Semi-retirement ===
[[File:Crystal Palace Centre transept & north tower from south wing.jpg|alt=A black and white photo of a massive glass building with a tower to the right and fountains in front. Below, in cursive script, is a caption that reads "Centre transept & north tower from South Wing"|thumb|The Crystal Palace in 1854]]
To make up for the subsequent loss of income, he accepted an invite to become a director of the [[The Crystal Palace|Crystal Palace Company]] on 30 December 1869;<ref name="obituary" /><ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=60}} of its eight original board members, two were also board members of the LB&SCR.<ref name="crystal-palace">{{cite web |last=Harrison |first=Melvyn |title=Leaving Hyde Park, 1851 |url=http://www.crystalpalacefoundation.org.uk/history/leaving-hyde-park-1851-2 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250420072602/http://www.crystalpalacefoundation.org.uk/history/leaving-hyde-park-1851-2 |archive-date=20 April 2025 |access-date=7 June 2025 |website=The Crystal Palace Foundation}}</ref>{{Efn|These were [[Samuel Laing (science writer)|Samuel Laing]] and [[Leo Schuster]].<ref name="crystal-palace"/>}} He also took an active part in his role at the Crystal Palace Company; in May 1870 he travelled to continental Europe in advance of the creation of the Crystal Palace Aquarium,<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=60}} which he would become heavily involved in the construction and management thereof.<ref name="obituary" /> The project, which was led by the aquarist [[William Alford Lloyd]],<ref name="crystal-palace-park-trust">{{Cite web |title=The Park's History |url=https://www.crystalpalaceparktrust.org/topics/the-parks-history |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250417212358/https://www.crystalpalaceparktrust.org/topics/the-parks-history |archive-date=17 April 2025 |access-date=7 June 2025 |website=Crystal Palace Park Trust |language=en-GB}}</ref> involved building the first stand-alone [[aquarium]] outside of a zoo and by far the largest aquarium in the [[British Isles]] when it opened in 1871.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Kisling |first=Vernon N |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=NkZ1EAAAQBAJ |title=Zoo and Aquarium History: Ancient Animal Collections to Conservation Centers |publisher=[[CRC Press]] |year=2022 |isbn=978-1-000-58538-4 |language=en |chapter=2.13: Victorian Aquarium Craze to a New Aquarium Age}}</ref> However, it did not see financial success.<ref name="crystal-palace-park-trust" /> 

On 1 November 1870, Jacomb-Hood joined the board of the Anglo-Maltese Hydraulic Dock Company.<ref name="obituary" /><ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=60}} Based in [[Victoria Street, London|Victoria Street]], London, they were constructing a dock in [[Malta]] which used a hydraulic system to lift boats out of the water and into a [[dry dock]]. The dock was finished in 1873 at a cost of £143,000,<ref>{{Cite journal |date=2 August 1879 |title=Railway Times & Joint-Stock Chronicle |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=_Q5CAQAAIAAJ |journal=The Railway Times |language=en |location=London |publisher=Railway Times and Joint-Stock Chronicle Office |volume=42 |issue=2169 |pages=644 |via=[[Google Books]] |archive-date=22 August 2025 |access-date=27 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250822145126/https://books.google.com/books?id=_Q5CAQAAIAAJ |url-status=live }}</ref> which is equivalent to £{{Inflation|index=GBP|value=143000|start_year=1873|r=-3|fmt=c}} in {{Inflation year|index=GBP}},{{Inflation/fn|UK}} and Jacomb-Hood was sent to Malta to assist in the company's affairs in July 1872.<ref name="obituary" /><ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=60}} He left the board in May 1877.<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=61}}

[[File:Robert Jacomb-Hood 1872 voyage.png|alt=A map of the Atlantic ocean, with the Americas on the left and Eurasia on the right. A red path connects twelve numbered blue dots as described in the image caption.|thumb|Jacomb-Hood's voyage to Argentina in 1872:<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=60}}{{Efn|All countries listed are the modern day jurisdictions for the locations, not necessarily those at the time. The locations (blue dots) are factual, but the path (red line) is only indicative of the rough route Jacomb-Hood would have taken.}}{{Ordered list|[[London]], UK|[[Dover]], UK|[[Calais]], France|[[Paris]], France|[[Lisbon]], Portugal|[[Dakar]], Senegal|[[Salvador, Brazil|Salvador]], Brazil|[[Rio de Janeiro]], Brazil|[[Buenos Aires]], Argentina|[[Montevideo]], Uruguay|[[Funchal]], Portugal|[[Southampton]], UK
}}]]
The Chairman of the LB&SCR, [[Samuel Laing (science writer)|Samuel Laing]], commissioned Jacomb-Hood to travel to [[Argentina]] and negotiate the purchase of the [[Central Northern Railway]] between [[Córdoba, Argentina|Córdoba]] and [[San Miguel de Tucumán|Tucumán]].<ref name="obituary" /><ref name="museo-ferroviario-2015">{{Cite web |year=2015 |title=Línea Belgrano |url=http://museo-ferroviario.com.ar/el-ferrocarril-en-argentina/linea-belgrano/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150214163837/http://museo-ferroviario.com.ar/el-ferrocarril-en-argentina/linea-belgrano/ |archive-date=14 February 2015 |access-date=7 June 2025 |website=Museo Ferroviario}}</ref> Jacomb-Hood incorporated the visit into a wider holiday of his between January and May 1872, which included visits to [[southeast Europe]], [[West Africa]], and the Atlantic coast of [[South America]] (see image for full route). He arrived back in the United Kingdom at [[Southampton]] on 13 June. Upon his return, he gave up his last Westminster office, which closed on 8 July 1872.<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=61}} The railway was eventually bought by the British-owned [[Córdoba Central Railway]] in 1887.<ref name="museo-ferroviario-2015" />
[[File:1891 Poor's Queen and Crescent Route.jpg|alt=A bordered map on cream paper captioned "MAP OF THE / QUEEN & CRESCENT / ROUTE." On the map is a dense network of railway lines with a y-shaped boldened line running from the bottom-left to top right|left|thumb|An 1891 map of the Alabama and Great Southern Railway Company; Jacomb-Hood was instrumental in its creation.]]
In 1877, he was commissioned by [[Emile Erlanger & Co.]] to assist in their decision whether or not to purchase the Alabama and Chattanooga Railway. He travelled to the United States with [[Sir Philip Rose, 1st Baronet]] in order to inspect the line and write a report on it.<ref name="obituary" /><ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=61}}It was as a result of this report that the [[Alabama Great Southern Railway Company]] was formed in 1877,<ref name="obituary" /> and he served as a director of the company from September 1877 until 1886, helping in its setup alongside Charles Schiff.<ref name="obituary" /><ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=61}} In 1879, he resigned as a board member of the Crystal Palace Company; the same year, he was commissioned for a second time by Emile Erlanger & Co., this time to assist with the company's undertakings in the [[West Indies]] and the United States.<ref name="obituary" /> 

In 1876, the Thames Haven Petroleum Storage Company had begun building warehouses to store barrels of petroleum oil from the [[Thameshaven|Thameshaven port]]. It began operating in 1880,<ref name="cracknell-1952">{{Cite journal |last=Cracknell |first=B. E. |year=1952 |title=The Petroleum Industry of the Lower Thames and Medway |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/40564775 |journal=Geography |volume=37 |issue=2 |pages=81 |issn=0016-7487 |jstor=40564775 |url-access=subscription |via=[[JSTOR]] |archive-date=7 June 2025 |access-date=6 June 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250607111638/https://www.jstor.org/stable/40564775 |url-status=live }}</ref> with Jacomb-Hood having been a director since November 1877;<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=61}} it maintained a monopoly on the import of petroleum into London until 1900.<ref name="cracknell-1952" /> Charles Schiff had also convinced Jacomb-Hood to join the board of the New Gas Company in September 1878.<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=61}}

In October 1879, he collaborated once again with the same people who had commissioned the [[Wealden Line]] between Lewes and Uckfield; this time, he was asked to help plan a restart of the never-completed [[Ouse Valley Railway]].<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=61}} The scheme never came to light and no work has been undertaken on the line since February 1869, when the LB&SCR made an agreement with the South Eastern Railway that rendered the line useless.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Robbins |first=Michael |date=September 1951 |title=The Ouse Valley Railway |url=https://www.bluebell-railway.co.uk/bluebell/history/OuseValleyRly.pdf |url-status=dead |journal=[[The Railway Magazine]] |pages=629 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250815172123/https://www.bluebell-railway.co.uk/bluebell/history/OuseValleyRly.pdf |archive-date=15 August 2025}}</ref> In November 1879, there were mass resignations among the board of directors at the Crystal Palace Company, including the Chairman, Deputy Chairman, and Jacomb-Hood;<ref>{{Cite book |last=Skinner |first=Thomas |author-link=Sir Thomas Skinner, 1st Baronet |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=wOcNAAAAQAAJ |title=The Stock Exchange Yearbook for 1880 |publisher=Cassell, Petter, Galpin & Co. |year=1880 |edition=6th |location=London |pages=247–248 |language=en-GB |archive-date=15 August 2025 |access-date=15 August 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250815211157/https://www.google.co.uk/books/edition/The_Stock_Exchange_Year_book/wOcNAAAAQAAJ?hl=en&gbpv=0&kptab=editions |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last=Skinner |first=Thomas |author-link=Sir Thomas Skinner, 1st Baronet |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=Bu4NAAAAQAAJ |title=The Stock Exchange Yearbook for 1882 |publisher=Cassell, Petter, Galpin & Co. |year=1882 |edition=8th |location=London |pages=344 |language=en-GB |archive-date=15 August 2025 |access-date=15 August 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250815213429/https://www.google.co.uk/books/edition/_/Bu4NAAAAQAAJ?hl=en&gbpv=0 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=61}} the following March he also left the board of the Thames Haven Petroleum Storage Co. In May 1880, he joined the board of the [[Sydney and Louisburg Railway]];<ref name="obituary" /><ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=61}} situated in [[Nova Scotia]], Canada, the company's main role was to carry coal mined by the [[Dominion Coal Company]], but also carried passengers. The only extant remains of the railway are the station building in [[Louisbourg]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Sydney and Louisburg Railway Station |url=https://www.historicplaces.ca/en/rep-reg/place-lieu.aspx?id=7813 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250616154419/https://www.historicplaces.ca/en/rep-reg/place-lieu.aspx?id=7813 |archive-date=16 June 2025 |access-date=15 August 2025 |website=[[Canadian Register of Historic Places]]}}</ref>

In May 1881, Jacomb-Hood submitted an entry to the design competition for [[Liverpool Exchange railway station]], something he had been working on since December of the previous year.<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=61}} On 3 August 1881, it was announced that John West had won the competition out of forty-three entrants, with the first part of the new station opening on 23 February 1888.<ref>{{cite book |last=Marshall |first=John |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=R7MMAQAAIAAJ |title=A Biographical Dictionary of Railway Engineers |publisher=[[David & Charles]] |year=1978 |isbn=978-0-7153-7489-4 |location=Newton Abbot |pages=79–84 |language=en-GB}}</ref> He also served as a director of the [[Assam Railways and Trading Company]] between August 1881 and February 1882,<ref name="obituary" /><ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=61}} a British company that later became part of the Indian [[Dibru–Sadiya Railway]]. It was commissioned to build a line in modern-day [[Assam]] on 16 July 1883, which eventually closed on 12 December 2011.<ref>{{cite news |date=12 December 2011 |title=State's First Railway Track Closed Down |url=http://www.assamtribune.com/scripts/detailsnew.asp?id=dec1311/at06 |url-status=usurped |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120111080342/http://www.assamtribune.com/scripts/detailsnew.asp?id=dec1311%2Fat06 |archive-date=11 January 2012 |accessdate=7 June 2025 |work=[[The Assam Tribune]] |location=Doom Dooma}}</ref> 

=== 1883–1900: Later work and death ===
[[File:LB&SCR B1 192 (Jacomb-Hood).jpg|thumb|[[LB&SCR B1 class]] locomotive number 192, named in honour of Jacomb-Hood|left|alt=A black and white photograph of a locomotive and its coal-wagon facing rightwards at a station platform. On the wheel casing reads "JACOMB HOOD".]]
On 7 March 1883, Jacomb-Hood was invited to join the LB&SCR's [[board of directors]] as a replacement for the retiring [[Arthur Otway|Sir Arthur Otway]];<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=62}} in the seventeen years between his appointment to the board and his death, he spent most of his time working on the LB&SCR's business.<ref name="obituary" /> His early work as a director involved the restructuring of the engineering department at the railway and assisting on individual projects such as [[Newhaven Harbour railway station]].<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=63}} 

William Jacomb—the cousin of former partner of Jacomb-Hood—died suddenly on 26 May 1887; Jacomb-Hood was present at his funeral on 31 May the same year, which took place at [[Wimbledon Cemetery]].<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=62}} He gave up the seats he held on the boards of the Alabama Great Southern Railway Company and the Sydney and Louisburg Railway in 1886, ending his involvement in engineering in [[Americas|the Americas]] that he had held since 1872.<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|pages=61–62}} Beginning in 1887, Jacomb-Hood also started attending the International Railway Congress; he was present at [[Milan]] in September 1887, [[Paris]] in September 1889, [[Brighton]] in October 1891 (of particular significance as the southern terminus of the LB&SCR<!-- This is not OR or speculation; it is mentioned in the source -->), and Paris in June 1893.<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|pages=62–63}}[[File:George Percy Jacomb-Hood (1857-1929) - Portrait of an Elderly Gentleman - 1977-5823 - National Railway Museum.jpg|thumb|upright|''Portrait of an Elderly Gentleman'' is believed to depict Robert Jacomb-Hood and was painted by his son George.|alt=A colour painting of a mostly bald man leaning against a desk. He is holding a cane in his left hand and has his right hand on his hip. He is wearing a long coat and red jumper and looks stern and directly forward.]]The LB&SCR dedicated one of their [[LB&SCR B1 class|B1 class]] locomotives (known as "[[William Ewart Gladstone|Gladstones]]") to Jacomb-Hood; number 192, named "Jacomb-Hood",<ref name="searle-2018">{{Cite web |last=Searle |first=David |date=2018 |title=Stroudley Locomotives: 0-4-2 Gladstone |url=https://www.lbscr.org/Rolling-Stock/Locomotives/Stroudley/Gladstone.xhtml |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250317172920/https://www.lbscr.org/Rolling-Stock/Locomotives/Stroudley/Gladstone.xhtml |archive-date=17 March 2025 |access-date=28 July 2025 |website=The London, Brighton & South Coast Railway}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last=Treloar |first=Peter |date=2006 |title=Stroudley's Gladstones |url=https://steamindex.com/archive/rlyarch2.htm |journal=Railway Archive |issue=14 |pages=8 |issn=1477-5336 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250117092303/https://steamindex.com/archive/rlyarch2.htm |archive-date=17 January 2025 |via=SteamIndex}}</ref> was an [[0-4-2]] locomotive that entered service in November 1888. The name was removed {{Circa|1906}} and the locomotive left service in December 1927.<ref name="searle-2018" /> The final year of his life in which he kept known diaries was 1894, by which point he was barely working and instead spent most of his time on holidays around the UK and Europe;<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=63}} he had only first visited mainland Europe on 29 May 1849, showing the extent of his financial success.<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=53}} He was present at the opening of the [[Tower Bridge]] in London on 9 July 1894, the last recorded association of his with a civil engineering project during his lifetime.<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=63}} 

The {{Circa|1895}} painting ''Portrait of an Elderly Gentleman'' by George Percy Jacomb-Hood is believed to be a depiction of Robert Jacomb-Hood, his father. It is currently held in the archives of the [[National Railway Museum]],<ref name="national-railway-museum" /> having been "obtained as a result of a direct claim of redundant material from the nationalised railway in 1977".<ref>{{Cite web |title=Portrait of an Elderly Gentleman |url=https://artuk.org/discover/artworks/portrait-of-an-elderly-gentleman-9452 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250527174323/https://artuk.org/discover/artworks/portrait-of-an-elderly-gentleman-9452 |archive-date=27 May 2025 |access-date=14 August 2025 |website=[[Art UK]] |language=en-GB}}</ref> Little is known about the last six years of Jacomb-Hood's life beyond his [[obituary]].<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=63}}

Jacomb-Hood died suddenly in [[Royal Tunbridge Wells|Tunbridge Wells]] on 10 May 1900, aged 78. At the time, he was still working for the LB&SCR, and was also one of the longest serving members of the Institution of Civil Engineers, having been elected over 53 years before his death.<ref name="obituary" /> His grave is inscribed with [[2 Timothy 4|2 Timothy 4:7]] as translated in the [[King James Version]]: "I have fought a good fight, I have finished my course, I have kept the faith".<ref>{{Cite web |year=1611 |editor-last=Bancroft |editor-first=Richard |editor-link=Richard Bancroft |title=2 Timothy 4:7 |url=https://biblehub.com/2_timothy/4-7.htm |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250525020025/https://biblehub.com/2_timothy/4-7.htm |archive-date=25 May 2025 |access-date=9 August 2025 |website= |publisher=[[King James Version]] |via=Bible Hub}}</ref><ref group="‡">{{Cite web |title=Robert Jacomb Hood |url=https://www.ancestry.com/search/collections/60526/records/2245274 |url-access=subscription |access-date=9 August 2025 |publisher=Find a Grave Index, 1300s–present |via=[[Ancestry]] |archive-date=31 August 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250831094315/https://www.ancestry.com/search/collections/60526/records/2245274 |url-status=live }}</ref> His obituary—published by the Institution of Civil Engineers—praised his dedication to the LB&SCR, saying that "he spared no pains to place at their service that keen intellect, fine memory and tactful discrimination for which he will be long remembered".<ref name="obituary" />

== Personal life ==
[[File:George Percy Jacomb-Hood - Self Portrait NPG 6083.jpg|thumb|upright|A [[self-portrait]] of Jacomb-Hood's son George|alt=A profile view of a young man with his head turned to face the viewer. He is wearing a blazer and bow tie and has short cropped hair and a moustache. It is a colour oil painting. ]]On 25 November 1851,<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Urban |first=Sylvanus |author-link=Edward Cave |year=1852 |title=The Gentleman's Magazine |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=U1RIAAAAYAAJ |journal=[[The Gentleman's Magazine]] |language=en-GB |location=London |publisher=W. Pickering |volume=37 |pages=183 |via=[[Google Books]] |archive-date=7 June 2025 |access-date=7 June 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250607111638/https://books.google.com/books?id=U1RIAAAAYAAJ |url-status=live }}</ref> Jacomb-Hood married Jane Stothard Littlewood. She was the daughter of George Littlewood, who was a local printer.<ref name="ellisrees-2021" /> The pair had met earlier the same year, because she was the governess of his cousin's children.<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=54}}According to their marriage certificate, they were married at [[St George's, Bloomsbury|St George's]] in the [[Bloomsbury|parish of Bloomsbury]] in [[Middlesex]] (now [[London]]). He is described as a civil engineer and his father a "[[gentleman]]"; neither had been previously married.<ref group="‡">{{Cite web |date=25 November 1851 |title=London, England, Church of England Marriages and Banns, 1754–1940 |url=https://www.ancestry.com/search/collections/1623/records/2530996 |url-access=subscription |access-date=12 August 2025 |via=[[Ancestry]]}}</ref> 

Jacomb-Hood had nine children with his first wife, of whom two died in infancy; their first child Katie (who later took the surname Dale by marriage) was born on 27 April 1853.<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=54}} Their fourth child, [[George Percy Jacomb-Hood]],<ref name="sellingantiques" /> was a successful painter and illustrator, and his work included depictions of his father.<ref name="national-railway-museum">{{Cite web |last1=Somerville-Woodiwis |first1=Wendy |last2=Marshall |first2=Helena |date=8 June 2016 |title=Surveying the Arts Store |url=https://blog.railwaymuseum.org.uk/surveying-the-arts-store/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241208092624/https://blog.railwaymuseum.org.uk/surveying-the-arts-store/ |archive-date=8 December 2024 |access-date=6 June 2025 |website=[[National Railway Museum]] |language=en-GB}}</ref><ref name="ellisrees-2021">{{Cite web |last=Ellis-Rees |first=William |date=1 August 2021 |title=The Art of Survival: Jacomb-Hood and the George |url=https://london-overlooked.com/jacomb-hood/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240422161927/https://london-overlooked.com/jacomb-hood/ |archive-date=22 April 2024 |access-date=6 June 2025 |website=London Overlooked |language=en-GB}}</ref> His second son, [[John Wykeham Jacomb-Hood]], also became a civil engineer; his career included working as the Chief Engineer of the [[London and South Western Railway]] and being elected to the Institution of Civil Engineers like his father.<ref>{{Cite journal |year=1915 |title=Obituary. John Wykeham Jacomb-Hood, 1859–1914 |url=https://www.emerald.com/jmipi/article/199/1915/452/417513/OBITUARY-JOHN-WYKEHAM-JACOMB-HOOD-1859-1914 |journal=Minutes of the Proceedings of the Institution of Civil Engineers |volume=199 |issue=1915 |pages=452–453 |doi=10.1680/imotp.1915.16234 |eissn=1753-7843 |via=[[Institution of Civil Engineers|ICE Virtual Library]] |archive-date=22 August 2025 |access-date=29 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250822174745/https://www.emerald.com/jmipi/article/199/1915/452/417513/OBITUARY-JOHN-WYKEHAM-JACOMB-HOOD-1859-1914 |url-status=live }}</ref>

On the death of his father on 9 July 1857,<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Urban |first=Sylvanus |author-link=Sylvanus Urban |year=1857 |title=Obituary |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=ePoIAAAAIAAJ |journal=[[The Gentleman's Magazine and Historical Review]] |language=en |location=London |publisher=John Henry and James Parker |volume=203 |via=[[Google Books]] |archive-date=8 August 2025 |access-date=8 August 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250808213818/https://www.google.co.uk/books/edition/The_Gentleman_s_Magazine_and_Historical/ePoIAAAAIAAJ?hl=en&gbpv=0 |url-status=live }}</ref> Jacomb-Hood inherited the family seat at Bardon Hall in Bardon, Leicestershire. On 13 June 1864,<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=58}} he sold the whole estate to William Percy Herrick of [[Beaumanor Hall]] for £40,000,<ref name="bulletin-of-the-loughborough-and-district-archaeological-society-1981" /><ref name="nuttall-1907">{{cite book |last=Nuttall |first=Gertrude Clarke |author-link=Gertrude Clarke Nuttall |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=pN-uIgAACAAJ |title=A Guide to Leicester and District |publisher=Edward Shardlow |year=1907 |location=Leicester |page=188 |language=en-GB |via=[[Google Books]] |archive-date=7 June 2025 |access-date=7 June 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250607111638/https://books.google.com/books?id=pN-uIgAACAAJ |url-status=live }}</ref> equivalent to £{{Inflation|index=UK|value=40000|start_year=1864|r=-4|fmt=c}} in {{Inflation/year|index=UK}}.{{Inflation/fn|UK}} However, the conditions of his inheritance was that this money would be split equally among him and his siblings.<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=58}} He was the second and last member of his family to own the estate.<ref name="walford" /><ref name="nuttall-1907" />

On 27 November 1869, Jacomb-Hood's wife Jane, to whom he had been married for 17 years, died of "dropsical debility",<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=60}} which refers to illness caused by an [[edema|oedema]], a fluid-filled swelling beneath the skin.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Gerrard |first1=Imogen |last2=Root |first2=Kelsie |date=Summer 2017 |title=Dropsy |url=https://library.leeds.ac.uk/special-collections/collection/2156 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250423233152/https://library.leeds.ac.uk/special-collections/collection/2156 |archive-date=23 April 2025 |access-date=11 August 2025 |website=[[University of Leeds]]}}</ref> On 12 August 1870, Jacomb-Hood became engaged again to Elizabeth Thornton and on 21 February 1871 they married at [[St George's, Hanover Square]],<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=60}} in the [[St George Hanover Square|eponymous parish]] in Middlesex (now London). His [[marriage certificate]] once again listed him as a civil engineer; however, his now-dead father was listed as an "[[esquire]]" rather than simply a "[[gentleman]]". Owing to the death of his former wife, he is described as a "[[Widow|widower]]"; his wife was listed as a [[spinster]], meaning that she had not been married previously.<ref group="‡">{{Cite web |date=21 February 1871 |title=Westminster, London, England, Church of England Marriages and Banns, 1754–1935 |url=https://www.ancestry.com/imageviewer/collections/61867/images/61865_314054001181_17990-00039 |url-access=subscription |via=[[Ancestry]] |access-date=11 August 2025 |archive-date=16 August 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250816235917/https://www.ancestry.com/imageviewer/collections/61867/images/61865_314054001181_17990-00039 |url-status=live }}</ref> Elizabeth died suddenly in 1875 from cancer;<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=61}} according to the inscription on her grave, she died on 10 January aged 46.<ref group="‡">{{Cite web |title=Elizabeth Jacomb-Hood |url=https://www.ancestry.com/search/collections/60526/records/2312940 |url-access=subscription |access-date=9 August 2025 |publisher=Find a Grave Index, 1300s–present |via=[[Ancestry]]}}</ref> He did not marry again, remaining a widower for the last twenty-five years of his life.<ref name="jackson-1996" />{{Reference page|page=61}}

